--TEST--
`execute` answers a `Core\Db\Write` rather than the count `PDO::exec` returns, so what a write did is read off one object with three questions on it — `rule:core-classes/db-statement-members`, spec § 18
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Db\Connection::execute
// `PDO::exec` answers an `int` and `lastInsertId` reads a second number off the
// connection afterwards, so "how many rows" and "what key" are two unrelated
// readings a caller has to sequence correctly. `rule:core-classes/db-statement-members` answers both from
// the statement that produced them, which is why `execute` has a return type of
// its own rather than a count.
var $db = Core\Db::connect("main");
var $write = $db->execute("insert into accounts (owner) values (?) returning id", ["ada"]);

// The three readings § 18's Results table gives a `Write`, as their own types.
// They are members and not properties: nothing a `Core` class owns has a
// property a program can reach.
uint $affected = $write->affected();
?uint $changed = $write->changed();
?uint $lastId = $write->lastId();

// And the boundary: the count is *on* the answer and is not the answer, so the
// spelling the PHP corpus trained is refused rather than quietly meaning
// something narrower.
uint $rows = $db->execute("delete from accounts", []);
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `uint`, found `Core\Db\Write`
   --> case.nvs:21:14
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
